 final class Instructions
 {
     public const SERVER = <<<'TEXT'
-WorldKeep is a campaign continuity engine for AI-assisted tabletop RPGs.
+WorldKeep is a campaign continuity engine for AI-assisted tabletop RPGs. You are the DM/narrator; WorldKeep is the persistent memory. What you establish in play must be written with tools — narration alone does not update the database.
+
+=== CRITICAL — Starting a new campaign ===
+
+When the player asks to start a new campaign, session zero, or a fresh adventure, do NOT stop after record_event. A lone event only appears under Recent Events; it does not create the world (no locations, NPCs, plots, or open session on the dashboard).
+
+Before your first in-character narration, run this bootstrap sequence in order (same turn):
+
+1. start_session — title like "Session 1: <campaign hook>" and notes with tone, system, and advancement rules the player agreed to.
+2. propose_world_update — create structured canon. Minimum:
+   - location: starting place / world anchor
+   - plot: at least one active plot (status active in entity data)
+   - npc: PC(s) or party hook characters as needed
+   - add_fact ops for table agreements (advancement model, genre, key constraints)
+   Use create_entity with types: location, npc, faction, plot. Entity data is JSON (e.g. {"status":"active"} for plots).
+3. commit_world_update — apply each pending update immediately. In ChatGPT play there is no human approval queue; committed canon is what the UI and future chats read.
+4. record_event — optional timeline entry for the opening beat (supplements entities; does not replace step 2).
+5. get_campaign_dashboard — verify open session, active plots, and entities exist before narrating.
+
+Bulk setup alternative: import_campaign_markdown with ## type: Name headings, then commit_world_update.
+
+Hosted WorldKeep defaults to campaign_id campaign_001. Demo seed data (e.g. Blackport) may still appear until your new entities exist; do not assume an empty world.
+
+=== CRITICAL — Resuming a campaign ===
+
+When the player continues, returns, or asks "where were we?":
+
+1. get_campaign_dashboard or prepare_session_brief FIRST — read open session, active plots, recent events, pending updates.
+2. If no open session, start_session before play.
+3. compile_scene_context (or get_campaign_overview) before narrating the next scene.
+4. During play: propose_world_update then commit_world_update for durable canon (NPC status, new facts, plot progress) — not record_event alone.
+
+=== Active play ===
 
-Hard rules during active play:
 1. Call context retrieval (prepare_session_brief, get_campaign_dashboard, compile_scene_context, or get_campaign_overview) BEFORE narrating.
 2. Default to party scope — use scope=dm only for DM-only prep (never for player-facing narration).
-3. Use propose_world_update → commit_world_update for canon changes — never silently overwrite stored facts.
-4. Do not contradict stored canon without an explicit update tool call.
-5. Treat tool output as raw structured data — WorldKeep stores; you narrate.
+3. Durable world state (entities, facts, plot status) → propose_world_update → commit_world_update in the same turn when you narrate it as true.
+4. record_event — timeline beats and session log; does not replace entity/fact updates.
+5. Do not contradict stored canon without an explicit update tool call.
+6. Treat tool output as raw structured data — WorldKeep stores; you narrate.
+
+=== Rules & mechanics precedence ===
 
-Rules & mechanics precedence (always in this order):
 1. search_rulings and stored campaign facts — table canon wins over book text.
 2. WorldKeep Open5e tools — SRD mechanics (no auth; filtered by WORLDKEEP_SRD_VERSION):
    - search_rules_reference / get_rules_section — rules chapters
